    "summary": "Lovelight opener, tight bass, soaring Mason's Children jam",
    "blurb": "The concert launches with a crisp Turn On Your Lovelight that immediately energizes the band. Highlights include a strong Alligator transition into The Other One, an extended Mason's Children jam, and standout bass clarity throughout. The soundboard capture is well‑balanced, though monitor problems shorten the acoustic Black Peter segment.",
    "review": "On February 28, 1970 at the Family Dog on the Great Highway, the Dead delivered a set that feels both adventurous and tightly knit. Opening with a bright Turn On Your Lovelight, Jerry’s lead work was crisp and energetic, setting up a relaxed acoustic sandwich that featured an intimate Black Peter before diving back into electric territory. The second half builds momentum through Alligator and a powerful drum dialogue between Mickey and Bill, culminating in a marathon Mason's Children jam that showcases the band’s improvisational depth. Phil’s bass shines with exceptional clarity and taught precision, anchoring the group while Pigpen adds his usual color on keyboards and harmonica. The soundboard tape captures the performance with excellent dynamics and instrument definition—especially the low‑end—though monitor issues trim a few moments of the acoustic segment. Overall, this show stands out for its inventive set order, strong rhythm section, and memorable jams.",
